<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Tim Herpich</title><link>https://tim-herpich.github.io/</link><description>Recent content on Tim Herpich</description><generator>Hugo -- gohugo.io</generator><language>en</language><copyright>© 2026 Tim Herpich</copyright><lastBuildDate>Tue, 10 Feb 2026 00:00:00 +0000</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://tim-herpich.github.io/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Solvency II Review: Rethinking Long-Term Guarantees</title><link>https://tim-herpich.github.io/posts/2026/02/solvency-ii-review-rethinking-long-term-guarantees/</link><pubDate>Tue, 10 Feb 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://tim-herpich.github.io/posts/2026/02/solvency-ii-review-rethinking-long-term-guarantees/</guid><description>&lt;div style="margin-bottom: 40px;"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div style="font-size: 80%;"&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;&lt;i class="fas fa-balance-scale"&gt;&lt;/i&gt; Introduction and Motivation&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We recently published a whitepaper on the Solvency II Review, focusing on the new Long-Term Guarantees (LTG) measures — in particular interest-rate extrapolation and the volatility adjustment.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>My CFA Journey: From Curiosity to Charterholder</title><link>https://tim-herpich.github.io/posts/2025/10/my-cfa-journey-from-curiosity-to-charterholder/</link><pubDate>Thu, 30 Oct 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://tim-herpich.github.io/posts/2025/10/my-cfa-journey-from-curiosity-to-charterholder/</guid><description>&lt;div style="margin-bottom: 40px;"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div style="font-size: 80%;"&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;&lt;i class="fas fa-lightbulb"&gt;&lt;/i&gt; How It Began&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;My journey with the Chartered Financial Analyst (CFA) Program began out of curiosity rather than ambition. I wanted to understand financial systems more deeply, and the CFA curriculum appeared to offer a structured path. At the time, I had little idea of the scope, discipline, and time that the program would require. What started as an interest in learning soon became a multi-year process of structured study and reflection.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Dealing with the Non-Marketable: Export Credit Insurances</title><link>https://tim-herpich.github.io/posts/2024/10/dealing-with-the-non-marketable-export-credit-insurances/</link><pubDate>Thu, 03 Oct 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://tim-herpich.github.io/posts/2024/10/dealing-with-the-non-marketable-export-credit-insurances/</guid><description>&lt;div style="margin-bottom: 40px;"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div style="font-size: 80%;"&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;&lt;i class="fas fa-balance-scale"&gt;&lt;/i&gt; The Challenge&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Export Credit Agencies (ECAs) insure risks that commercial insurers avoid: long-term, high-risk, and often politically uncertain transactions. While pricing export credit insurance policies is already complex, pricing &lt;em&gt;modifications&lt;/em&gt; to these policies is an even greater challenge. There are no regulatory guidelines, no established market benchmarks, and each export case exhibits unique characteristics and therefore idiosyncratic risk profiles.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>MBA: Lessons Beyond the Books</title><link>https://tim-herpich.github.io/posts/2024/08/mba-lessons-beyond-the-books/</link><pubDate>Tue, 20 Aug 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://tim-herpich.github.io/posts/2024/08/mba-lessons-beyond-the-books/</guid><description>&lt;div style="margin-bottom: 40px;"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div style="font-size: 80%;"&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;&lt;i class="fas fa-graduation-cap"&gt;&lt;/i&gt; The MBA Journey&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Embarking on the two-year &lt;a href="https://www.bwl.uni-mannheim.de/en/"&gt;Mannheim Business School&lt;/a&gt; MBA journey has been a truly transformative experience — both personally and professionally. What stood out most was the uniquely diverse and international environment, where classmates from varied professional, cultural, and personal backgrounds came together. This diversity enriched every discussion and constantly challenged my perspectives.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Beyond Beta: Hunting for the Perfect Portfolio</title><link>https://tim-herpich.github.io/posts/2024/01/beyond-beta-hunting-for-the-perfect-portfolio/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://tim-herpich.github.io/posts/2024/01/beyond-beta-hunting-for-the-perfect-portfolio/</guid><description>&lt;div style="margin-bottom: 40px;"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div style="font-size: 80%;"&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;&lt;i class="fas fa-history"&gt;&lt;/i&gt; Six Decades of Capital Market Research&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The question of what constitutes an optimal investment portfolio has been at the heart of capital market research for decades. Traditionally, the Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM), developed by William Sharpe and based on Harry Markowitz’s Modern Portfolio Theory, posits that the market portfolio—a fully diversified basket of all investable assets—represents the most efficient investment. According to CAPM, expected returns depend solely on systematic market risk (beta), meaning individual investors cannot achieve excess returns through stock selection or market timing. However, empirical studies have repeatedly challenged this assumption, leading to the emergence of factor investing.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title> An expert session on pricing issues</title><link>https://tim-herpich.github.io/talks/items/an-expert-session-on-pricing-issues/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://tim-herpich.github.io/talks/items/an-expert-session-on-pricing-issues/</guid><description/></item><item><title> Collective Effects in Stochastic Thermodynamics</title><link>https://tim-herpich.github.io/publications/items/collective-effects-in-stochastic-thermodynamics/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://tim-herpich.github.io/publications/items/collective-effects-in-stochastic-thermodynamics/</guid><description/></item><item><title> Stochastic Thermodynamics of Coupled Phase Oscillators</title><link>https://tim-herpich.github.io/talks/items/stochastic-thermodynamics-of-coupled-phase-oscillators/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://tim-herpich.github.io/talks/items/stochastic-thermodynamics-of-coupled-phase-oscillators/</guid><description/></item><item><title>Collective Power: Minimal Model for Thermodynamics of Nonequilibrium Phase Transitions</title><link>https://tim-herpich.github.io/publications/items/collective-power-minimal-model-for-thermodynamics-of-nonequilibrium